  "slug": "disorder-chern-plateaus-7d05",
  "summary": "Preprints show disorder and point defects can generate, enhance or tune orbital responses, while a clean-limit MoS2 study isolates orbital exchange effects.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that disorder and defects act as sources or tuning knobs for orbital transport rather than mere perturbations. Kubo-Bastin Chebyshev calculations on a centrosymmetric sp3 square lattice address robustness and tunability of an orbital Hall effect with quadrupole origin under disorder. Structural and electrostatic disorder, including amorphous graphene, is reported to produce sizable orbital Hall currents absent in pristine systems. A real-space study finds that point defects enhance and activate magnetoelectric orbital responses in a metallic orbital antiferromagnet. Two items differ in scope: the toroidal spin texture work treats dipole-dipole interaction landscapes in a classical magnetostatic model, and the MoS2 study derives intrinsic anomalous Hall conductivity from an orbital exchange field without spin magnetism or spin-orbit coupling.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
